#X obj 60 40 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty d50.i~\ and\ d50~ 24 6 0 22 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 76 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Roland\ D-50\ linear\ arithmetic\ synthesizer 24 6 0 16 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 120 d50.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty d50.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 d50.i~ is the whole instrument in one object: d50~ \, the editor \, the controller layer \, the Launchkey pads and display \, MIDI in and out. Put it in a patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the editor \, see Editor. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1) \, argument 2 the channel of the pads (0 or none = 10 \, the Drums Channel). Several d50.i~ do not get in each other's way \, each has its own editor. Page 1 of the controller is the performance row \, then the selected partial and its tone: page k is row k of the editor. \; d50~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: inlet 2 takes the velocity of a note sent as a float \, inlet 3 raw MIDI bytes from midiin (notes \, bend \, aftertouch \, program change \, CC 1 \, 5 \, 7 \, 64 \, 65 and SysEx) \, and the floats of outlet 3 (SysEx) go to midiout. Open d50.i~ to see the wiring., f 64;
#X obj 60 504 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 532 The editor is the window of d50.i~. Every change shows up in it \, from the controller \, a patch change \, messages or SysEx. refresh asks for all values again. \; Rows of 8 below the two bands \, titled as in Controls: rows 1 to 11 are the pages of the controller layer \, the rows after them have no CC (mouse only). Each cell: name and label \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value as on the D-50. \; PARTIAL SELECT: the D-50 has four partials \, two per tone. The PARTIAL rows (blue) show and change the selected one \, U1 U2 L1 L2 \, the TONE rows (beige) the tone it belongs to \, upper for U1 and U2 \, lower for L1 and L2. It is the control part \, first cell of page 2 \, so the controller can switch it too. upper tone \, lower tone: their names. \; PERFORMANCE (row 1) acts on the whole patch: cut moves the cutoff of every synth partial \, also of notes that are sounding. res \, att \, rel and vib shift resonance \, attack \, release and vibrato of all partials for the notes that follow. These are not stored and go back to 0 with every patch change. The other controls of the row are patch values \, volume is the master. \; An edit of a partial or tone value is heard from the next note on. Chorus \, equalizer \, reverb and the balances follow at once. The built-in presets cannot be edited: load a bank \, or start from init or a user slot. \; PATCH band. name: type a new patch name and press enter \, up to 18 characters (upper and lower tone: 10). factory N: a factory preset \, numbered through all .syx banks beside d50~. user N: a user slot \, 0 to 63. reload: the same patch again \, edits dropped. init patch: a plain sawtooth. store to N: chooses the user slot \, save patch stores the playing patch there and writes the user bank \, see Presets. load bank \, write bank: read or write the bank file without storing. send bank \, send patch: as SysEx to MIDI out \, see SysEx. \; MIDI in: the receive channel \, 0 = all. out: the channel of the controller feedback and the device number of SysEx dumps., f 64;
#X obj 60 1122 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1150 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; d50cc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of d50~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; d50pads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. d50~ has no own pads yet. \; Storing asks first: save once shows Store to? and the user slot played or stored last (savearm) \, user + / - choose the slot (saveslot 1 / -1 \, nothing is recalled) \, save again stores there and writes the user bank (save). Any other pad or 10 s cancel (savecancel). Own pads and init after its first press show what they do on the display. \; Pad colours \, sent by d50pads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user and save green (the user bank) \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save flashing while it asks and white for a moment when stored \, own pads purple \, free pads off. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 1628 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1656 1 float: note number \, velocity 100. \; 1 list: note \, velocity and \, if you like \, the MIDI channel. \; 1 anything: a message \, see Messages., f 64;
#X obj 60 1718 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1746 1 signal: left output. \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, name \, 0-127 and value text for every \;   change \, the patch that plays \, its names \, the channels., f 64;
#X obj 60 1824 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets\ and\ user\ bank 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1852 preset selects a factory preset \, user a slot of the user bank. The third outlet answers with the number and the name. A patch change sets cut \, res \, att \, rel and vib back to 0. \; The user bank (64 slots) is d50user-bank.syx in the folder of your patch \, so every project keeps its own. d50.i~ reads it at load if it is there \, otherwise the bank beside d50~ is the starting point. save \, userwrite and userread without a file name use this file: store to N in the editor chooses the slot \, save patch stores and writes. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. \; There is one user bank in Pd \, not one per object: two d50.i~ in one patch share it. \; tune and voices (SETUP) belong to the instrument and are not stored in a patch., f 64;
#X obj 60 2090 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2118 name v: set a control \, in the values the D-50 shows. \; name: report it as info. \; n name v: set it from a pot or a MIDI CC \, 0 to 127. \; ninfo name: report it as info. \; part n: the partial that partial names act on \, 0 to 3 \;   (U1 \, U2 \, L1 \, L2). \; preset n: a factory preset \, read only. \; user n: a slot of the user bank \, 0 to 63. \; init: a plain sawtooth patch to start from. \; reload: the same patch again \, edits dropped. \; store n: the playing patch into user slot n \, in memory. \;   Without n: the user slot played or stored last. \; save: store \, then write the user bank (the pads' save). \; savearm \, saveslot n \, savecancel: the pads' store with a \;   question: show the slot \, move it \, drop it. save after \;   savearm stores into the slot shown. \; presetstep [n] \, userstep [n]: the factory patch or user \;   slot n after the one played last \, wrapping (the pads). \; userwrite [file] \, userread [file]: the user bank \, see \;   Presets. A file name without a path is beside d50~. \; usersend: the user bank as SysEx. \; presets \, users: list the names. \; l2 cutoff 30: section \, name and value \, without changing \;   the selection. Sections: u1 u2 upper l1 l2 lower patch. \;   Without a value it reports the value. \; param a v: one of the 448 patch bytes by address \, the \;   section times 64 plus the offset. param a reports it. \; patch name My Patch: the patch name \, up to 18 characters. \;   upper name \, lower name: up to 10. \; dump: every named parameter of every section as info. \; sysexdump: the playing patch as SysEx. \; sysex bytes: one SysEx message from a list. \; bend f: pitch wheel \, -1 to 1 \, times bendrange. \; mod f: modulation lever \, 0 to 1. \; aftertouch f: channel pressure \, 0 to 1. \; sustain 0/1: hold pedal. \; channel n: MIDI receive channel \, 0 is all. \; txchannel n: channel of the controller feedback and device \;   number of SysEx dumps \, 1 to 16. \; governor 0/1: when 1 \, the oldest released note ends early \;   whenever a block takes too long. Held notes never end. \;   For small machines such as Bela. Off by default. \; status: report patch \, names \, channels \, voices and CPU. \; stop: all notes off. \; reset: all notes off and the effect tails emptied., f 64;
#X obj 60 2852 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2880 A Roland D-50 in one object: linear arithmetic synthesis with two tones of two partials each. A partial is a synth partial (oscillator \, filter \, amplifier) or a PCM sample \, structure sets which and whether they are ring modulated. Each patch has its own chorus \, equalizer and reverb \, the output is stereo. Eight voices per tone. \; keymode sets how the tones play: whole \, dual \, split and their solo variants. The engine runs at 32 kHz \, the rate of the original \, and is resampled to the rate of Pd., f 64;
#X obj 60 3038 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty SysEx 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3066 The object speaks the exclusive format of the D-50 \, so an editor or librarian made for the D-50 can program it \, and it can exchange patches with a real D-50 \, over MIDI in and out. \; Data set (DT1) to address 00-00-00 edits the playing patch \, the same 448 bytes that param reaches. Data set to 02-00-00 \, the internal memory of the D-50 \, goes to the user bank: a bulk dump replaces it in memory \, userwrite keeps it. \; A request (RQ1) for either area is answered at MIDI out. sysexdump sends the playing patch and usersend the user bank without being asked. The device number of incoming messages is not checked \, answers carry the number of the request., f 64;
#X obj 60 3256 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Data\ files 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3284 The object contains no Roland data. d50~ loads its files from its own folder \, or else from the folder of the patch that holds it. For d50.i~ that patch is d50.i~.pd itself \, so put the files beside d50~. \; d50_pcm.bin: the PCM samples \, written by tools/make_d50_data.py from your own ROM images. Without it the object stays silent and says so in the Pd window. \; Every .syx file beside it is read as a factory bank \, a D-50 bulk dump of 64 patches \, in alphabetical order \, PND50-00.syx first \, numbered through. Without one the object plays eight built-in presets \, which cannot be edited. \; d50user-bank.syx beside d50~: a user bank in the same format \, the starting point for d50.i~ (see Presets). Until it has been written it holds 64 init patches. \; A D-50 dump ends with the data of its reverb types 17 to 32. The object does not use them \, its reverbs are built in \, but it keeps the block of the dump it came with and puts it into every bank it writes or sends \, so a D-50 gets a complete dump back. \; For d50.i~: d50~ \, d50.i~.pd and the data files in a folder on Pd's search path., f 64;
#X obj 60 3634 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3662 In the order of the editor: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range \, CC number and what it does., f 64;
#X obj 60 3740 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 1\ \ PERFORMANCE\ \ CC\ 20-27 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3768 cut (Cutoff all) -63..63 \, CC 20: cutoff of every synth \;   partial \, also of notes that are sounding. 0 leaves the patch \;   as it is \; res (Reso all) -63..63 \, CC 21: resonance of every synth \;   partial \, for the notes that follow \; att (Attack all) -63..63 \, CC 22: attack (TVA time 1) of all \;   four partials \, for the notes that follow \; rel (Release all) -63..63 \, CC 23: release (TVA and TVF time 5) \;   of all four partials \, for the notes that follow \; vib (Vibrato all) -63..63 \, CC 24: vibrato (LFO pitch \;   modulation) of both tones \, for the notes that follow \; chorus (Chorus) 0..100 \, CC 25: chorus balance of both tones \; balance (Tone balance) 0..100 \, CC 26: balance of the two tones \; volume (Volume) 0..100 \, CC 27: master volume \, not stored in a \;   patch, f 64;
#X obj 60 4022 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 2\ \ PARTIAL\ WG\ \ CC\ 28\ 29\ 30\ 31\ 33\ 34\ 35\ 36 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4050 part (Partial) 0..3 \, CC 28: the partial the partial names act \;   on \, and with it the tone (U1 U2: upper \, L1 L2: lower) \; coarse (Pitch coarse) 0..72 \, CC 29: pitch in semitones \, 0 is \;   C1 \; fine (Pitch fine) -50..50 \, CC 30: fine tune \; keyfollow (Pitch KF) 0..16 \, CC 31: pitch keyfollow step \, 11 is \;   a semitone per key \; wave (Waveform) 0..1 \, CC 33: synth waveform: square \, sawtooth \; pcm (PCM wave) 1..100 \, CC 34: PCM sample \; pw (Pulse width) 0..100 \, CC 35: pulse width \; pwlfodepth (PW LFO) 0..100 \, CC 36: pulse width LFO depth, f 64;
